@page
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Projects & Sprints — Guide";
}
<div class="page-header">
    <div class="section-head section-head--plain">
        <h1>Projects &amp; Sprints</h1>
        <p class="page-context">Plan and organize delivery work.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="zone">
    <p class="prose">
        Projects are a lightweight way to plan delivery work on top of the same tickets you
        already use everywhere else in FlowOps — there is no separate work item type for
        planned work.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-flow">
        <div class="guide-flow__step">Project</div>
        <span class="guide-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="guide-flow__step">Sprint</div>
        <span class="guide-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="guide-flow__step">Board</div>
        <span class="guide-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="guide-flow__step">Work</div>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-term-grid">
        <div class="guide-term">
            <p class="guide-term__name">Project</p>
            <p class="guide-term__body">A container for related tickets, with its own overview, ticket list, and sprints.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-term">
            <p class="guide-term__name">Sprint</p>
            <p class="guide-term__body">A dated slice of a project's work. Only one sprint is active at a time; others are Planned, Completed, or Cancelled.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-term">
            <p class="guide-term__name">Board</p>
            <p class="guide-term__body">The active sprint's tickets, grouped by status (Planned, Open, In Progress, Pending, Done). Move a card with the mouse or its own menu — both do exactly the same thing.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="prose">
        Planning a sprint means adding tickets to it from the project's ticket list — a ticket
        keeps its real workflow status the whole time, whether or not it has been moved onto
        the board yet. Completing a sprint freezes a record of what was done and what wasn't;
        unfinished tickets are not moved automatically, but can be carried forward into the next
        sprint. Cancelling a sprint before it starts releases its planned tickets back to the
        project. Every project keeps its own sprint history, so past sprints stay visible after
        they're done.
    </p>

    <p class="page-actions">
        <a class="btn-primary" asp-page="/Projects/Index">Open Projects</a>
    </p>
</div>

<div class="guide-section-nav">
    <a class="back-link" asp-page="/Guide/Index">&larr; Back to Guide</a>
</div>
